# THE FOUR NEW FIXTURES REPORT `UNSHAPED` AT THE PREVIOUS PIN, WITH ZERO
# CONSTRAINT CHECKS, and that is a stronger RED than the `NO_WARNING` the two
# `.WARN.` ones would give if they were merely unconstrained. An extended
# profile carries no `rdf:type` of its own, so at core v3.8 there is no shape in
# the entire published set that reaches an untyped `<#me>` at all: nothing could
# have noticed the empty family name or the second given name, because nothing
# was looking at the node. A `--select 'core/extended-profile*'` run at that pin
# does not even complete — the runner's own self-check aborts with "zero
# constraint checks evaluated across the entire suite", which is the same fact
# stated by the harness rather than by a verdict. All four pass here at 9
# constraint checks each.
#
# ONE MEASUREMENT WORTH RECORDING SEPARATELY, because it is evidence about the
# corpus rather than about the new fixtures. `cascade:ExtendedProfileShape`
# targets the SUBJECTS OF `cascade:dateOfBirth`, and `cascade:PatientProfile`
# nodes carry that property too, so the shape reaches eight pre-existing
# fixtures as well: profile-001, -002, -003 and -005 go 32 → 41, 76 → 85, 32 →
# 41 and 32 → 41 constraint checks, and the four genomics/phenopackets oracles
# go 53 → 62, 164 → 191, 193 → 220 and 100 → 109. Every one of the eight keeps
# its verdict and its reason exactly — the four profile fixtures still pass, the
# four phenopacket oracles still fail for the same `genomics:` reasons they
# failed for before. That is the release's claim that a `cascade:PatientProfile`
# node is also reached and passes unchanged, exercised eight times rather than
# asserted once. (profile-004 carries no `cascade:dateOfBirth` and stays at 32.)
